## The questions most likely to challenge the story

**1. How do you know isolation works, and not just that the model refused?**
Three things. The detector looks for the planted canary and its fragments in the raw model output, not for refusals. The same probes recover a canary when context is shared on purpose: {{e1_positive.history}} with shared history and {{e1_positive.gateway}} through a context-sharing gateway ({{e1_positive.label}}). With nothing planted: {{e1_negative.text}} ({{e1_negative.label}}). Evidence: `{{e1_isolation.ev}}`.

**2. What do the zero-leak results establish, and what not?**
Direct adapter: {{e1_isolation.sessions}} and {{e1_isolation.trials}} recovered another session's canary ({{e1_isolation.label}}). The one-sided 95% upper bound is {{e1_bound.sessions}} and {{e1_bound.trials}} ({{e1_bound.label}}). That bounds the leak rate for these probe wordings, this model, this server build and these flags. "No leak detected" is never "no leak". Full pipeline (broker, sandboxes, real model, E2): {{e2_isolation.runs}}, {{e2_isolation.attacks}}, {{e2_isolation.red+}}; in the context-sharing control the model leaked in {{e2_control.runs}} and the broker withheld {{e2_control.withheld}}, {{e2_control.red+}}. Prompt cache (E3): cache off, cached tokens in {{e3_cache_off.text+}}; cache on (control) {{e3_cache_on.auc+}}; cache off, latency {{e3_latency_off.auc+}}. Evidence: `{{e1_bound.ev}}`, `aegislab/evidence/finale_p4_2026-11-11/README.md`.

**3. Why did the 250 ms timing result change?**
With the stub model, 250 ms response buckets bring a latency classifier to chance: {{timing_stub.acc250}} ({{timing_stub.label}}). The stub answers in near-constant time. A real model's latency spans many 250 ms buckets, and refusals never reach the model, so with the real model the refusal decision stayed readable: {{timing_p3_single.acc250}} in one execution ({{timing_p3_single.label}}), confirmed in three balanced executions: {{timing_250_real.acc}}, {{timing_250_real.p}} ({{timing_250_real.label}}). Our own harness found this; the old "padding brings it to chance" statement now always says "stub model". Evidence: `{{timing_p3_single.ev}}`, `{{timing_250_real.ev}}`.

**4. Why {{deadline_default.D_eq+}}, and what does deadline release cost?**
Every reply, answered or refused, leaves at t0 + D; a model that has not answered by D minus the reserve is cut off and red gets the uniform refusal. D was calibrated per execution from the measured latency: {{timing_deadline_cost.D_each}} ({{timing_deadline_cost.label}}); the default is the largest, {{deadline_default.D_eq}} ({{deadline_default.label}}, `aegislab/aegis/runs.py`, overridable with `AEGIS_TIMING_DEADLINE_MS`). Result: {{timing_deadline_real.acc}} against majority {{timing_deadline_real.base}}, {{timing_deadline_real.p}} ({{timing_deadline_real.label}}). Cost: {{timing_deadline_cost.cost}} per answered request and {{timing_deadline_cost.cutoff}} of model calls cut off ({{timing_deadline_cost.label}}). D is host- and model-specific; another machine must re-calibrate (`aegislab/evaluation/timing_deadline.py`, phase one).

**5. One deadline p-value is below 0.05. Why is that not a leak?**
The third execution gave {{timing_deadline_real.p3}} ({{timing_deadline_real.label}}). We declared before measuring that a leak needs p below {{timing_deadline_real.alpha}} ({{timing_deadline_real.label}}) in any execution (Bonferroni over nine tests). It is below 0.05 and above that threshold, so the verdict is "no leak detected (inconclusive as to absence)". We did not move the threshold and we show the number. Evidence: `{{timing_deadline_real.ev}}`.

**6. What if the broker or the signing key is compromised?**
A compromised broker can sign a false but consistent record; the verifier cannot tell. The finale work moved the key out of the containers' reach: every container runs as {{f4_userns.inside}} on its own host UID, never the broker's {{f4_userns.broker}}, and a container that mounts the key gets {{f4_userns.errno}} ({{f4_userns.label}}; `{{f4_userns.ev}}`). The key is still an unencrypted 0600 file of the host user: a kernel exploit that becomes that user can forge evidence. The reviewer countersignature refuses a broker-key forgery, but a forger holding both keys still succeeds (security demo step 7, {{security_demo.limits}}, LIVE). So the countersignature is **Partial**: it helps only while the reviewer key lives on another machine. KMS/HSM and rotation: future work. Evidence: `docs/finale/P4_REPORT.md` section 5, `aegislab/THREAT_MODEL.md` section 10, `finale_package/terminal/t3_security_demo.txt`.

**7. Containers share the kernel. Did you try gVisor or Kata?**
No. `runsc` is not installed on the test host and the finale rules allowed no downloads, so gVisor is "not tried", not "failed". A kernel exploit from a tenant container defeats the boundary; with the F4 split it lands on a subordinate UID that cannot read the key, but a further escalation to the host user reaches it. Evidence: `docs/finale/P4_REPORT.md` OPTIONAL 9, `aegislab/THREAT_MODEL.md` row 8.

**8. Does the canary detector catch encoded leaks?**
Single encodings, yes, since the finale work: base64, hex, rot13, reversed, split and spelled-out forms of another run's canary are withheld, and the live demo shows a base64-only leak withheld ({{real_model_demo.checks}}, LIVE). Still missed: double encodings, other bases, ciphers, homoglyphs, translation, paraphrase, and a token split across replies. The detector is the second line; server-side isolation (stateless requests, cache off) is the first. Evidence: `aegislab/aegis/canary_scan.py`, test `P3B02d` (double encoding, LIMIT), `docs/finale/P4_REPORT.md` MUST 3.

**9. How many models did you test?**
One at full scale: Qwen2.5-0.5B Q4_K_M ({{e1_isolation.label}}). One reduced run with Qwen2.5-1.5B (E1 and E3 only): {{second_model.trials}}, {{second_model.sessions}}, {{second_model.controls}} ({{second_model.label}}, `{{second_model.ev}}`). Both on CPU with llama.cpp. Larger models, other servers and other builds are untested.

**10. GPU?**
Untested. Everything ran on CPU. GPU memory, accelerator KV-cache and CUDA-context isolation are not claimed. Evidence: `aegislab/THREAT_MODEL.md` section 9.

**11. Are the anomaly detectors tuned?**
No. {{detectors.n}} ({{detectors.label}}) have fixed thresholds chosen from the scripted agent's behaviour and written rationales (`aegislab/aegis/anomaly.py`), not tuned on adversarial traffic. An attacker who stays below every threshold is not flagged. Detectors never change a reply, and alerts go into the signed log without opening a channel to red. Evidence: `docs/finale/P4_REPORT.md` section 4.

**12. Can a capability token be revoked?**
Not yet. Tokens are run-scoped, operation-scoped and expire within an hour; only the reviewer issues reviewer tokens, so the operator cannot mint the right to release; there are no delegation chains. There is no revocation list, and the API has no TLS (it is loopback-only). Evidence: `aegislab/aegis/captoken.py`, `docs/finale/P4_REPORT.md` SHOULD 6.

**13. Why is the public demo read-only?**
A live operator console on a public URL would hand run control (create, start, release) to anyone with the link. So https://aegislab.vercel.app runs with `AEGIS_PUBLIC_DEMO=1` forced in `aegislab/app.py`: no launch link, no session, every write refused except bundle verify and tamper on a disposable copy, live execution off, rate-limited per client and overall. Visitors can verify a real bundle and watch a tampered copy fail. Status: the hosted site serves `origin/main`, where Verify still fails with HTTP 403; the fix (P1) goes live when the finale branches are merged. Evidence: `docs/finale/P1_REPORT.md`, `aegislab/tests/test_finale_p1_public_demo.py`.

**14. Is the real model really live in the demo?**
Yes. `scripts/real_model_demo.sh` and the console started with `scripts/start_real_model_console.sh` use llama.cpp in a rootless container with no network, and nothing falls back to the stub: {{real_model_demo.checks}} and the browser run {{console_check.checks}} (LIVE). The run card shows "REAL MODEL · LIVE" with the model file and its sha256 prefix. Transcripts: `finale_package/terminal/t1_real_model_demo.txt`, `finale_package/terminal/t6_real_model_console_check.txt`.

## Integrity of the evidence

**What does strict verification check?**
Chain, checkpoint signatures, the signed terminal checkpoint binding the final entry and state, a pinned key and terminal hash, and, if required, the reviewer countersignature. Recorded live on the presented code: strict verify {{verify_cli_live.ok}}, a one-field edit of a copy {{verify_cli_live.bad}} (LIVE, `finale_package/terminal/t5_verify_cli.txt`). Tamper suite: {{tamper.categories}} detected in {{tamper.trials}}; CLI {{tamper.genuine}}, {{tamper.tampered}} ({{tamper.label}}, `{{tamper.ev}}`).

**Access control?**
Role, operation and state matrix: {{role_matrix.attempts}}, {{role_matrix.denied}}, {{role_matrix.allowed}}, none unexpectedly permitted ({{role_matrix.label}}, `{{role_matrix.ev}}`). API matrix: {{api_matrix.denied}}, {{api_matrix.traversal}} rejected ({{api_matrix.label}}).

**Can red signal blue through the monitoring stream?**
Not any more. Participants and the public see run-level lifecycle events only until the run is SEALED; per-attack alerts and refusal reasons go to the operator and reviewer only, and the stream never carries a red-chosen string. Remaining: counts and lifecycle timing are visible. Evidence: `docs/finale/P2_REPORT.md` F3, `aegislab/THREAT_MODEL.md` row 9.

**Can a tenant flood the broker?**
Per-tenant mailbox quotas: {{quotas.text}} ({{quotas.label}}). Over any limit the tenant is cut off, `mailbox.quota_exceeded` is recorded, and the run seals incomplete. Evidence: `aegislab/aegis/runs.py` `MailboxQuota`.

## Problem fit, feasibility, reproduction

**Which Track 04 requirement does AegisLab address?**
All six evaluation objectives: isolation that keeps the system working, leakage prevention, audit integrity, deployability, LLM-native threats, and quantified residual risk. The weakest remaining areas are host-level key custody and hardware isolation, both on the residual-risk slide.

**Why not just separate containers?**
Containers are the primitive. You also need a single mediated channel whose identity cannot be spoofed, a configuration frozen before attacks run, attacks committed in advance, stateless model calls, results sealed until release, and evidence an outsider can verify.

**Is the deployment reproducible?**
One Fedora laptop, rootless Podman, no root daemon. The sandbox base image is pinned by digest, the model file and the llama.cpp server binary are checked by sha256 before use, and `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock` were corrected in finale P4. Nothing is downloaded at the venue. Evidence: `docs/finale/P4_REPORT.md` MUST 5, `aegislab/Containerfile`, `aegislab/aegis/runtime.py`.

**Can I reproduce it?**
`cd aegislab && bash scripts/quickstart.sh`, then `bash scripts/judge_demo.sh` ({{judge_demo.checks}}, LIVE, stub model, seconds, offline), `bash scripts/security_demo.sh` ({{security_demo.checks}}, LIVE), and `.venv/bin/python evaluation/check_claims.py` ({{check_claims.total}}, LIVE). The real-model demo needs the model and server files next to the repository (`_models/`, not committed). Tests on this branch: {{tests.passed}}, {{tests.browser}} ({{tests.label}}).

**What is not claimed?**
Production readiness, a third-party audit, invulnerability, model robustness to prompt injection, GPU or physical KV-cache isolation, protection against a compromised broker, any customer, partnership or certification.
